<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Re</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ra, also called Re, was the ancient Egyptian god of the Sun, order, kings and the sky. By the Fifth Dynasty, in the 25th and 24th centuries BCE, he had become one of the most important gods of Egyptian religion, identified primarily with the noon-day Sun. According to Egyptian myth he carried the Sun across the sky in his solar barque by day and sailed through the Duat, the underworld, by night, defeating the serpent Apophis before returning at dawn. He was usually shown as a man with a falcon's head crowned by a solar disc, and he was merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> as Ra-Horakhty and, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> as Amun-Ra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ra.jpg" alt="Ra" width="330" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ra_Enthroned_in_the_Tomb_of_Roy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ra's local cult began to grow from roughly the Second Dynasty. Its chief centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heliopolis-ancient-egypt">Iunu</a>, &quot;the Place of Pillars&quot;, later called Heliopolis and today in the suburbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, where he was identified with the local sun god Atum. As Atum or Atum-Ra he was reckoned the first being and the originator of the Ennead, the Nine. By the Fourth Dynasty pharaohs were seen as his manifestations on Earth, called Sons of Ra.</p><p>In accounts of creation the sun god existed alone in the watery mass of Nun, and the Benben, a pyramid-shaped mound, emerged from it. A lotus grew with the mound, and Ra came out of it when it opened. He was believed to have created all forms of life by calling them into existence with their secret names.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Ra was usually shown as a man with the head of a falcon, crowned with a sun disc encircled by a cobra. He could also appear as a beetle as Khepri, as a ram, or as a phoenix, heron, serpent, bull, cat or lion. In some texts he is an aging king with golden flesh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> bones and hair of lapis lazuli.</p><p>By day he carried the Sun across the sky in his solar barque, the Mandjet. At dusk he passed through the western horizon into the Duat and sailed the subterranean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> through twelve gates on the Mesektet, in his ram-headed form. Each night the serpent Apophis, god of chaos, attacked the boat, and Ra's companions, among them Sia, Hu and Heka, helped to defeat it before he returned at dawn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Worship increased greatly in the Fifth Dynasty, when Ra became a state deity and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> had pyramids, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> and sun temples built in his honour. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a> gave him growing importance in the dead king's journey through the Duat. In the Middle Kingdom he was increasingly combined with other chief gods, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> tomb walls carried detailed texts on his journey through the underworld. The rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> ended his worship.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
